[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Runtime dependencies enter through Nix

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-DEPS-VIA-NIX
TITLE: Runtime dependencies enter through Nix
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: A runtime dependency is added by any other route and the reason is recorded here.
STATEMENT: >>>
Every runtime dependency of this repository's tooling -- Python packages, Node
packages, tree-sitter grammars, binaries -- enters through Nix, into the
runtime that uses it. The shapes that count: a nixpkgs package spliced onto the
interpreter that needs it, python3.withPackages, or a wrapper that puts the
dependency on the program's path. What is ruled out: a user-global install, pip
or npm -g into an ambient environment, and a lockfile resolved outside Nix at
build or run time.

A package manager such as uv may be used to manage a set of dependencies,
provided the manager itself is Nix-installed and its output is reproducible
from the flake. The rule is about where the bytes come from and whether another
machine gets the same ones, not about which tool arranges them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Anyone on another machine gets the same runtime, and a dependency cannot go
missing because a developer's global environment happened to carry it. The two
instances already live in the tree: the tree-sitter grammars and transitions
reach the strictdoc venv through the PYTHONPATH splice in
packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix, and the board gets its
interpreter from grammarPython = python3.withPackages in devenv.nix. Neither
needs a user global and neither needs pip.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The operator, verbatim: "we should log a sdoc like a decision or mechanism for
adding deps (node, py, etc) is via nix. i would approve using like ux if its
easier to manage some of that but still nix installed."
<<<
